* START-HISTORY:
* 02 Oct 26 Windows port - split out of CREATEA for RELEASE_1.1 116:
*           RESTORE.ACCOUNT (shared with SD Core for Linux) creates an account
*           that is missing, and must not parse a sentence to do it.  The
*           signature and the settings positions (syscom/createacc.h, Linux's,
*           copied byte for byte) were agreed by mail 1 Oct 2026; each port
*           writes its own body.  The body below is CREATEA's, moved whole with
*           its comments, from the USER arm's checks on; every "stop
*           sysmsg(n, ...)" is now err = n, args and a goto exit.create (a
*           return inside a gosub, tested by its caller), following Linux's
*           split: never a goto out of a gosub, and the ACCOUNTS readu released
*           at exit.create because a subroutine gets no stop's implicit
*           release.  Earlier history is CREATEA's and stays there.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !create_account(acc.type, acc.name, settings, err)
*
*    acc.type  USER, GROUP or OTHER.
*    acc.name  As typed.  The account name and register key are folded to lower
*              case below, as CREATE.ACCOUNT always did.
*    settings  syscom/createacc.h.  Blank fields take CREATE.ACCOUNT's
*              defaults: both routes on for a USER account, no os.users record.
*              CA$REUSE.SD.USER (31) is honoured too, on the owner's ruling of
*              2 Oct 2026 - see AN EXISTING WINDOWS USER below.
*    err       '' when the account was made.  Otherwise the message number,
*              then its arguments, value-mark separated - the caller shows it
*              with sysmsg(err<1,1>, err<1,2>, ...).  Progress lines are
*              displayed here as CREATE.ACCOUNT always displayed them.
*
* THE GATES ARE CHECKED HERE, NOT ONLY IN THE VERB.  This is catalogued, so a
* program can call it without going through CREATE.ACCOUNT: the administrator
* check, and ATTACH's -internal and one-shot marker (consumed only here), are
* repeated.
*
* A NEW USER ACCOUNT ASKS FOR A PASSWORD.  NO.QUERY is refused for it (10039),
* because making the Windows user means setting its password; the prompt and
* its retry stay here, interactive, exactly as in CREATE.ACCOUNT.
*
* AN EXISTING WINDOWS USER IS REFUSED (10038) unless ATTACH, or unless the
* caller set CA$REUSE.SD.USER and SD itself made that user (!is_sd_user).  Owner,
* 2 Oct 2026: such a user is used again WITH A NEW PASSWORD - on Windows the API
* credential ($cred) is written together with the Windows password, so keeping
* the old one could leave ssh working and the API refused.  RESTORE.ACCOUNT sets
* CA$REUSE.SD.USER; CREATE.ACCOUNT never does.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine create_account(acc.type, acc.name, settings, err)
$catalog !create_account

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include err.h
$include createacc.h

deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun os_group(action,group,member) calling '!os_group'
deffun is_group(group) calling '!is_group'
deffun is_user(uname) calling '!is_user'
deffun is_sd_user(username) calling '!is_sd_user'
deffun create_user(uname) calling '!create_user'
deffun set_passwd(uname)  calling '!set_passwd'
deffun delete_user(uname) calling '!delete_user'
deffun profile_dir(uname) calling '!profile_dir'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun valid_os_path(path) calling '!valid_os_path'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

   err = ''
   locked = @false
   acc.f.open = @false

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      err = 2001  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
      return
   end

   if umask(002) then null

   acc.type = upcase(trim(acc.type))
   acc.uname = ''
   group_name = ''

*  The settings become the variables CREATE.ACCOUNT's body has always read.
*  A route field that is present at all is a request (CREATE.ACCOUNT's
*  access.given); blank means on, which is RELEASE_1.1 68's "silence means
*  BOTH".
   access.given = settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> # '' or settings<CA$ROUTE.API> # ''
   access.ssh = settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> # '0'
   access.api = settings<CA$ROUTE.API> # '0'
   no.query = settings<CA$NO.QUERY> = 1
   reuse.sd.user = settings<CA$REUSE.SD.USER> = 1   ;* owner, 2 Oct 2026 - see the USER arm
   os.sh   = settings<CA$OS.SH> = 1
   os.exec = settings<CA$OS.EXECUTE> = 1

*  ATTACH, re-checked here: -internal and the one-shot marker for THIS account
*  (RELEASE_1.1 66).  The verb refuses the word with 2018 when either gate is
*  shut, without naming it; a caller that bypassed the verb gets 2018 the same
*  way, and the marker is consumed only here.
   attach = @false
   if settings<CA$ATTACH> = 1 then
      attach.marker = @sdsys:@ds:'$attach.':downcase(acc.name)
      if acc.type = 'USER' and kernel(K$INTERNAL, -1) and ospath(attach.marker, OS$EXISTS) then
         attach = @true
         void ospath(attach.marker, OS$DELETE)
      end else
         err = 2018:@vm:'ATTACH'  ;* Unexpected token (xx)
         return
      end
   end

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      err = 6000 ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
      return
   end
   acc.f.open = @true

   begin case
   case acc.type = 'USER'
     pathname = CONFIG('USRDIR')
     if pathname = '' then pathname = 'C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts'
     acc.uname = acc.name

     * Is the user a valid os user?
     * Check reserved names then
     * Check user name as entered, then force to lower case and re check
     begin case
     
     * is user reserved?
     case upcase(acc.uname) = 'ROOT' 
       @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
       err = 6052 ; goto exit.create ;* User name is invalid
     
     case upcase(acc.uname) = 'SDSYS' 
       @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
       err = 6052 ; goto exit.create ;* User name is invalid
      
     case upcase(acc.uname) = 'SDUSERS' 
       @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
       err = 6052 ; goto exit.create ;* User name is invalid
          
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - THE TWO ADOPT BRANCHES ARE GONE.  They admitted any
*   pre-existing OS user, which is not the model: the repository owner's rule
*   of 14 Aug 2026 is that the ONLY pre-existing OS user that may be given an
*   SD account is the installer's, done at install time, and every other SD
*   account creates its own OS account from within SD.
*
*   The verb already refused, but by accident and with the wrong words.
*   !is_user read /etc/passwd, which MSYS2 does not have, so it answered false
*   for everyone, the adopt branches never ran, and control reached
*   create_user(), which fails on an existing account with "Create User
*   Failed, OS Error: 1".  Right outcome, unreadable reason - and it rested
*   entirely on a broken lookup, so repairing !is_user on its own would have
*   turned the refusal into a silent adoption of somebody's existing login.
*   Both were changed together; see PROJECT_STATUS.md section 6.
     case is_user(acc.uname) or is_user(downcase(acc.uname))
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE ADOPT EXCEPTION IS GONE WITH THE
*   KEYWORD, so this is the plain refusal it always was for everybody else: an
*   SD account is never attached to a Windows account that already exists.  The
*   install used to take that exception for the installing user; what replaces
*   it is the Windows SDSYS account, which is in the register from the start
*   and needs no CREATE.ACCOUNT at all.
*
* 18 Sep 26, LATER STILL - AND THE EXCEPTION IS BACK, AS ATTACH, BECAUSE THE
*   PARAGRAPH ABOVE ANSWERED THE ADMIN QUESTION AND NOT THE INSTALLER'S OWN.
*   The owner, the same day: "during the install, the regular sd account for
*   the installer needs to be created as well since you can't create an
*   account later because sd only recognizes accounts it creates."  SDSYS
*   covers administration; it does not give the PERSON who installed SD an
*   account, and this refusal is the wall that keeps them from ever getting
*   one.  The ATTACH keyword - install-only, marker-gated, in more.args - is
*   the one crossing, and it exists for exactly one caller:
*   gplbld/attach-account.ps1, at ssPostInstall, for the user who
*   authenticated the elevation prompt.  With attach set, this case MATCHES
*   (the premise is that the Windows account exists), folds the name, and
*   falls out of the begin case PAST create_user and set_passwd to the SD
*   half - no OS account is created and no password is set, the account
*   already being the user's.
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 67.  IT FOLDS UNCONDITIONALLY NOW, AND
*   THAT IS A DELIBERATE NARROW EXCEPTION TO THE LINE AT :695, NOT AN OVERSIGHT
*   BEING CORRECTED.  This read "if is_user(acc.uname) else acc.uname =
*   downcase(...)" - folding only in its else - so a name found under its typed
*   spelling was never folded.  FOR AN ATTACHED ACCOUNT THE MATCH IS THE
*   PREMISE, so it never folded at all, and the first witnessed install left
*   "sdsys\accounts\don" pointing at "user_accounts\Don" with group "sdu_Don".
*   THAT IS THE 22 Aug 2026 SYMPTOM MIRRORED - see :912, where the owner's
*   ruling was made "on seeing sdsys\accounts\DON sitting beside
*   user_accounts\don for the same person".  The register key folds at :937
*   whatever happens here, so leaving this unfolded guarantees the mismatch.
*
*   :695's COMMENT IS THE THING TO READ BEFORE CHANGING THIS BACK: "if you
*   don't want this, pre create the os user" - a pre-created OS account keeping
*   its own spelling is an ESCAPE HATCH somebody may have used on purpose, and
*   it is left alone.  ATTACH is not that case: the installing user did not
*   pre-create anything for SD's benefit, their Windows account merely predates
*   SD, so the hatch's rationale does not reach them and the only account it
*   would ever apply to is the installer's own.
*
*   SAFE BECAUSE THE WINDOWS LOOKUP IS CASE-INSENSITIVE, MEASURED RATHER THAN
*   ASSUMED: Get-LocalUser 'don' resolves the account Windows spells 'Don',
*   with 'donzzz' refused as the control.  So os_group("ADDMEM", ...) and the
*   sdu_ group built from acc.uname at :1193 and :1231 all reach the same
*   Windows account under the folded name.  Nothing on the attach path creates
*   an OS object under the typed spelling - create_user and set_passwd are both
*   skipped - so there is no second spelling left anywhere to disagree with.
*
*   THE MARKER IS UNAFFECTED: it is built at the acc.uname assignment in the
*   USER arm with its own explicit downcase(), before this point, so folding
*   here cannot move it.
*
*   ***DO NOT TAKE THIS LINE FROM THE LINUX TREE. IT IS THE ONE PLACE THE TWO
*   PORTS' createa MUST DIFFER, AND THEIR AGENT SAID SO IN WRITING (19 Sep
*   2026).***  They hit the same fault and fixed it a DIFFERENT way, because
*   LINUX OS USER NAMES ARE CASE-SENSITIVE: folding theirs unconditionally
*   would chown to a user that does not exist when the login really is "Don".
*   So their createa keeps the uid as the Linux user spells it - for chown,
*   sdusers and sdu_ membership - and folds only the account name, the
*   directory, the sdu_ group NAME and ACC$GROUP.  Windows has no such split
*   to make: the SAM is case-INSENSITIVE (measured above), so one fold serves
*   every use and the split would be dead weight here.  createa is shared
*   BASIC and both ports pull each other's shapes, so a future merge that
*   brings their arm across would not fail loudly - it would simply stop
*   folding the Windows name again and put RELEASE_1.1 67 back.
       begin case
         case attach
           acc.uname = downcase(acc.uname)

* 02 Oct 26 Windows port - CA$REUSE.SD.USER, OWNER'S RULING 2 Oct 2026 ("reuse it,
*   new password"): a RESTORE that has to create an account may take over a
*   Windows user of that name ONLY when SD itself made it (!is_sd_user reads the
*   stamp CREATE_USER writes, the same test DELETE.ACCOUNT trusts).  It asks for
*   a NEW password, because on Windows !set_passwd writes the Windows password
*   and SD's API credential ($cred) together: reusing the old password would
*   leave a user that ssh admits and the API refuses, if its $cred went with
*   the old account.  SD Core for Linux keeps the two apart and leaves the
*   password unchanged; that does not transfer (mail 2026-10-02T1615).
*   NO ROLLBACK HERE, unlike the creating arm below: this user existed before,
*   so a refused password leaves it exactly as it was, and nothing is made.
         case reuse.sd.user and is_sd_user(downcase(acc.uname))
           acc.uname = downcase(acc.uname)
           crt sysmsg(12007, acc.uname) ;* SD made %1 for an earlier account; used again, with a new password
PW.REUSE.AGAIN:
           if not(set_passwd(acc.uname)) then
              pw.stat = status()
              begin case
                 case pw.stat = 3 ; crt sysmsg(10118)          ;* The two passwords did not match
                 case pw.stat = 1 ; crt sysmsg(10119, acc.uname) ;* Windows refused that password
                 case pw.stat = 5 ; crt sysmsg(10120)          ;* Not elevated - retrying will not help
                 case pw.stat = 6 ; crt sysmsg(10122, acc.uname) ;* Windows set, $cred not
                 case pw.stat = 7 ; crt sysmsg(10920)          ;* A password needs at least 8 characters...
                 case 1           ; crt sysmsg(10121, pw.stat) ;* Could not be set (status %1)
              end case
              crt sysmsg(12008): ;* No new password was set.  Try again (y/<n>)?
              input yn
              if upcase(yn) = 'Y' then goto PW.REUSE.AGAIN
              @system.return.code = -ER$NOT.CREATED
              if pw.stat = 6 then
                 err = 10122:@vm:acc.uname ;* Windows set, $cred not - say exactly that
              end else
                 err = 12009:@vm:acc.uname ;* %1 was not given a new password, so the account was not made
              end
              goto exit.create
           end

         case 1
           @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
           err = 10038:@vm:acc.uname ; goto exit.create ;* Windows account %1 already exists
       end case

     case attach
*      ATTACH names an OS account that is not there, so there is nothing to
*      attach to.  Creating it instead would be the opposite of what was
*      asked - the account exists in Windows and the SD account must be bound
*      to THAT one, or `sd` at the person's console lands nowhere.
       @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
       err = 6074 ; goto exit.create ;*    Invalid user name

* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE 'ADOPT' CASE IS DELETED WITH THE
*   KEYWORD.  It read: ADOPT names an OS account that is not there, so there is
*   nothing to adopt, and creating it instead would be the opposite of what was
*   asked.  Nothing can reach it now, and "creating it" is the only behaviour
*   left - which is what the arm above refuses when the name already exists.
*   LATER THE SAME DAY: the case above IS that arm, restored as ATTACH - same
*   refusal, same reason, new name.

     case no.query
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - this branch used to be reachable only when the name
*   was not an existing user, and said "Invalid user name", which was already
*   the wrong reason: the name is fine, it is NO.QUERY that cannot work.
*   Creating an OS account means setting its password, and SET_PASSWD prompts.
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         err = 10039:@vm:acc.uname ; goto exit.create  ;* Cannot create %1 with NO.QUERY

     case 1
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - THE config('CREATUSR') GATE IS GONE.  Decision from
*   the repository owner: SD has accounts, not accounts and users as QM and
*   ScarletDME do, so creating an account IS creating the operating system
*   account and there is nothing to opt in to.  Asking permission to do the
*   second half of the only thing this verb does made no sense once the two
*   were the same thing.  The parameter still parses (config.c) and CONFIG
*   still prints it; nothing consults it here any more.
       * sd convention is os user names are lowercase, if you don't want this, pre create the os user
         acc.uname = downcase(acc.uname)

* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - LOOK BEFORE LEAPING.  Owner's ruling,
*   PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 36: a leftover profile directory on this name used to
*   produce a suffixed home SILENTLY, and the symptom that reached the user was
*   ssh public-key authentication refused - days later, nowhere near the cause,
*   because sshd reads authorized_keys under the home Windows assigned and the
*   key was under the other one.  Refusing converts a silent wrong answer into
*   an explained one.
*
*   AFTER THE downcase, NOT BEFORE.  The directory Windows will use is named
*   after the name this verb is about to create, and that is settled one line
*   above; testing the name as typed would ask about a path that will not be
*   used, and answer "clear" for a directory that is really in the way.
*
*   IT CANNOT CLEAR THE PATH AND DOES NOT PRETEND TO.  The hive is still
*   mounted, which is why the directory outlived its account; the message says
*   what has to happen instead.  !delete_user records the pair and the SD
*   service reclaims it at the next restart, so on a machine where SD made the
*   mess the answer is genuinely just "restart, then try again".
*
*   STATUS() IS READ, AND THAT IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING.  !profile_dir returns
*   empty both for "the path is clear" and for "PowerShell would not run", and
*   acting on the empty alone would report a clear path on a check that never
*   looked - the shape this project has paid for repeatedly.  A check that
*   could not run must not be scored as a pass, so it stops the creation too:
*   the directory is exactly the thing that cannot be undone afterwards.
         prof.dir = profile_dir(acc.uname)
         prof.stat = status()
         if prof.stat # 0 then
           @system.return.code = -ER$NOT.CREATED
           err = 10125:@vm:acc.uname:@vm:prof.stat ; goto exit.create ;* could not check for a leftover profile
         end
         if prof.dir # '' then
           @system.return.code = -ER$NOT.CREATED
           err = 10124:@vm:acc.uname:@vm:prof.dir ; goto exit.create ;* a profile directory is already there
         end

		 * Check INVERT/NOINVERT STATUS (1=INVERT on)
         PVAL = SYSTEM(1001)
         IF PVAL = 1 THEN
           CRT "Warning: **** Case Inversion is not active during password entry! ****"
         END
         if not(create_user(acc.uname)) then
           * create user failed
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - THE MINUS WAS MISSING, and it is the one error exit
*   in this file of twenty-five that did not have it.  gplsrc/err.h states the
*   convention at the top: "Commands that set negative error codes into
*   @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE use the arithmetic inverse of the values listed below."
*   So this left 6 where every other failure here leaves -6.
*
*   AND POSITIVE IS NOT AN UNUSED RANGE - it means "this many things were
*   done".  COPY and COPYP put records.copied there, DELETE and NSELECT put
*   record.count, GETLIST and FORMLST put a dcount.  A refused creation was
*   leaving behind a value indistinguishable from "six records copied", and
*   PROC:677 - "if @system.return.code < 0" - is how a PROC asks whether the
*   command it just ran failed.  It was being told this one had not.
*   Upstream too: UPSTREAM_FIXES.md #11.
           @system.return.code = -ER$NOT.CREATED
           err = 10006:@vm:STATUS() ; goto exit.create ;* Create User Failed, OS Error: %1
         end else
           crt sysmsg(10007,acc.uname)  ;* 'User ':acc.uname:' Created'
           * user created set passowrd
PW.TRY.AGAIN:
           if not(set_passwd(acc.uname)) then
           *  password failed
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - SAY WHICH FAILURE IT WAS.  10008 offered a retry and
*   named no reason, and the two likeliest causes need opposite responses: a
*   mistyped confirmation is fixed by typing it again, and a password Windows
*   REFUSED will be refused for ever no matter how carefully it is retyped.
*   Somebody hitting the second case could retype a matching pair all day.
*
*   !set_passwd ALREADY KNEW - the caller was throwing it away.  SET_PASSWD's
*   START-DESCRIPTION documents the values and it sets them with set.status
*   before every failing return: 1 Windows refused it, 2 not a valid OS user
*   name, 3 nothing entered or the two differed, 5 SD is not elevated.  Read
*   immediately, because anything else here would overwrite it.
*
*   THE RETRY IS LEFT ALONE except in the one case where it cannot work: a
*   session that is not elevated will not become elevated by answering Y, so
*   10120 says so rather than letting the loop offer it.  Changing the control
*   flow is a bigger question than this entry.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 22.
              pw.stat = status()
              begin case
                 case pw.stat = 3
                    crt sysmsg(10118) ;* The two passwords did not match
                 case pw.stat = 1
                    crt sysmsg(10119, acc.uname) ;* Windows refused that password
                 case pw.stat = 5
                    crt sysmsg(10120) ;* Not elevated - retrying will not help
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - status 6 is the HALF-SET case, and it is new because
*   the same prompt now sets both credentials.  Windows took the password and
*   $cred did not, which is the one failure that leaves an account ssh will
*   admit and the API will refuse - exactly the state PRE_RELEASE 42 was about.
*   The retry is left offered: unlike 5, a $cred write can fail transiently,
*   and answering N unwinds the whole account as it does for every other arm.
                 case pw.stat = 6
                    crt sysmsg(10122, acc.uname) ;* Windows set, $cred not
* 19 Sep 26 Windows port - status 7 is SD'S OWN REFUSAL, RELEASE_1.1 75, and it
*   is a third thing rather than a shade of 1.  Windows refusing a password
*   (1) cannot be fixed by retyping it; SD refusing it can be fixed by typing
*   a DIFFERENT one, and 10920 is the only message here that says what would
*   satisfy the rule.  The retry is left offered for exactly that reason.
                 case pw.stat = 7
                    crt sysmsg(10920) ;* A password needs at least 8 characters...
                 case 1
                    crt sysmsg(10121, pw.stat) ;* Could not be set (status %1)
              end case
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - WORDING ONLY, NO CODE CHANGE.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 79.
*   The test below is "= 'Y' then retry", so anything else - an empty answer
*   included - already falls through to the unwind.  The prompt now says so.
              crt sysmsg(10008): ;* 'Warning, user created but password not set, Retry (y/<n>)'
              input yn
              if upcase(yn) = 'Y' then goto PW.TRY.AGAIN
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - AND ANSWERING N NOW UNWINDS.  Owner's rule, 21 Aug
*   2026: an account cannot be created without a password.
*
*   IT USED TO CARRY ON.  The account was made, the message said "user created
*   but password not set", and creation ran to completion - leaving an SD
*   account whose Windows user has no password and therefore no $cred, which
*   under the SCRAM login is an account nothing can ever connect to.  A warning
*   is the wrong shape for a state that cannot be used.
*
*   THE UNWIND IS COMPLETE BECAUSE OF WHERE THIS SITS.  Only the Windows user
*   exists at this point: sdu_ group, account directory, VOC and the ACCOUNTS
*   record are all created further down.  So removing the user is the whole of
*   it, and delete_user takes any profile with it.
*
*   THE PASSWORD RULE IS THE USER ARM'S ALONE.  A GROUP account reaches neither
*   create_user() nor set_passwd() - it has no Windows user - and "no password"
*   there means the idea does not apply, not that a requirement was waived.
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - THE RESULT IS READ NOW.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 72.
*
*   IT WAS "void delete_user(acc.uname)" AND THE void WAS THE DEFECT.  10086
*   below says "Nothing was created", which is a CLAIM ABOUT WHAT JUST HAPPENED,
*   and it was printed whether or not the removal worked.
*
*   MEASURED 30 Aug 2026 ON THE OWNER'S VM.  A create.account run from a session
*   that reached SDSYS by LOGTO from an unelevated start failed at the $cred
*   write (PRE_RELEASE 68), the owner answered N, this line ran, and the Windows
*   account SURVIVED - in no group, so never confined by sdsshonly, and
*   invisible to remove-sdaccounts.ps1, whose candidate set is sdusers
*   membership.  It outlived the uninstall as an ordinary enabled login.
*   DELETE_USER's own os.execute -> ps_script change is the other half of this.
*
*   A FAILED ROLLBACK IS NOT A WARNING, IT IS A DIFFERENT OUTCOME, so it gets a
*   different message rather than a quieter version of the same one: 10086 keeps
*   its promise and is printed only when the promise is true.
              rollback.ok = delete_user(acc.uname)
              @system.return.code = -ER$NOT.CREATED
              if rollback.ok then
                 err = 10086 ;* An account must have a password. Nothing was created.
              end else
                 err = 10130:@vm:acc.uname
                 * The Windows account %1 could not be removed and is still there
              end
              goto exit.create
           end
         end
     end case
     
     
* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - uid and gid were saved here for set.owner, which has
*   gone.  ACC$GROUP is written from "sdu_":acc.uname directly, so nothing else
*   wanted them.
