* grant.os.access  -  write this account's OS.USERS record, if it is to have one
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - the ruling is at the call site.  os.sh and os.exec
* are set by "sh-on" and "os-on" in more.args and BOTH are set for an
* ADMINISTRATOR, so this routine does not test the tier itself: what it needs
* to know is already in the two flags, and asking twice is how two answers
* start to disagree.
*
* IT WRITES BOTH FIELDS EVEN WHEN ONLY ONE WAS ASKED FOR, "no" in the other.
* A record with one field is a record whose second field a later reader has to
* guess at, and both gates read them positionally.
*
* USER ACCOUNTS ONLY.  A GROUP account has no single person behind it and
* acc.uname is never assigned on that arm.
*
* AN EXISTING RECORD IS LEFT EXACTLY AS IT IS.  A "no" already in the file is
* somebody's decision and a create-time default has no business overruling it.
* It says so out loud rather than passing in silence, because "the
* administrator still cannot use the shell" is otherwise a question with
* nothing to read.
* ======================================================================

grant.os.access:
   if not(os.sh) and not(os.exec) then return
   if upcase(acc.type) # 'USER' then return
   if trim(acc.uname, ' ', 'B') = '' then return

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.f else
      display sysmsg(10104, acc.uname)
      * Unable to change operating-system access for %1
      return
   end

   read osu.rec from osu.f, acc.uname then
      close osu.f
      display sysmsg(10103, acc.uname)
      * %1 already has an os.users record, so it was left exactly as it was
      return
   end

   osu.sh = 'no'
   osu.ex = 'no'
   if os.sh   then osu.sh = 'yes'
   if os.exec then osu.ex = 'yes'
   osu.rec    = osu.sh        ;* field 1 - SH
   osu.rec<2> = osu.ex        ;* field 2 - OS.EXECUTE

   osu.written = @true
   write osu.rec to osu.f, acc.uname on error osu.written = @false
   close osu.f

   if osu.written then
      display sysmsg(10102, acc.uname, osu.sh, osu.ex)
      * os.users: %1 has SH %2, OS.EXECUTE %3
   end else
      display sysmsg(10104, acc.uname)
   end

   return

